    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d717">

                <pb facs="0923" n="911" sameAs="frus:frus1946v07/pg911"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.51/7–846: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">u.s. urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-07-10T11:00:00-04:00">July 10, 1946—11
                            a.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>497. 1. Embtel 741, July 8.<anchor n="42"
                        xml:id="d717-f1"/> Para 2 Deptel 492
                    stated Eximbank and NAC had approved $25 million exporter credits for Turkey. $19
                    million figure apparently garbled.</p>
                <p>2. Embtel 738, July 5.<anchor n="42"
                        xml:id="d717-f2"/> Fact that loan will be in form “exporter credits” will
                    still leave Turkish Govt free decide projects to be financed under loan, and no
                    projects will be financed under loan without approval Turkish Govt. Term
                    “exporter credit” means that Eximbank will
                    participate up to $25 million in financing projects put forward jointly by
                    Turkish Govt and US suppliers, or put forward by US suppliers with approval
                    Turkish Govt. In any event, since Turkish Govt notes or Turkish guarantee would
                    be required before Bank would make advances under credit, no advances could be
                    made without Turkish Govt’s approval.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
